<td id="S6.T2.6.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S6.T2.6.4.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">29.47</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To further analyze the performance of the streaming SSL model on the offline ASR task, we computed the WER based on the type of messages (see section <a href="#S3" title="3 ATC datasets used in this study ‣ In-domain SSL pre-training and streaming ASR:Application to Air Traffic Control Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).
Table <a href="#S6.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 6.4 SSL streaming models applied to offline ASR ‣ 6 Streaming ASR for ATC ‣ In-domain SSL pre-training and streaming ASR:Application to Air Traffic Control Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> presents the WER for controller, pilot, and ATIS messages in the Airbus-ATC test corpus.
An improvement is observed for each type of message, with the smallest occurring in ATIS messages (-1.37% relative) and the largest in the noisiest category, pilot messages (-3.46% relative).
These results suggest that the mixed SSL training approach using both full context samples and dynamic chunking is particularly useful to process noisy recordings.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.T3" class="ltx_table">
